    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051941</IRI>
        <Literal>Amyloid_Deposition</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="x_1.1:source"/>
            <Literal>UMLS</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051941</IRI>
        <Literal>Aβ protein deposition within the CNS is the primary cause of AD and an early critical event that promotes downstream features of the disease, such as NFT formation and synaptic injury and loss, leading to progressive dementia. 

https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-pathol-020712-163927</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:exactMatch"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051941</IRI>
        <Literal>Amyloid deposition</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="#UMLS_CUI"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051941</IRI>
        <Literal>C0011560</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="unti:MeSH_Unique_ID"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051942</IRI>
        <Literal>D058225</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="unti:Synonym"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051942</IRI>
        <Literal>Senile plaque; neuritic plaque</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051942</IRI>
        <Literal>Amyloid_Plaque</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="x_1.1:source"/>
            <Literal>UMLS</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051942</IRI>
        <Literal>One study has shown that SPs vary in size among both AD patients and normal elderly individuals; larger size distributions correlated with an earlier age of disease onset. SPs did not, however, appear to increase significantly in size over the clinical course of the disease.

https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-pathol-020712-163927</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="x_1.1:source"/>
            <Literal>UMLS</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051942</IRI>
        <Literal>Plaques consist of amyloid deposits surrounded by a web of astrocytic processes, swollen neurites, and neuron terminals; seen in the cerebral cortex in Alzheimer&apos;s disease.

From CSP</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:exactMatch"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051942</IRI>
        <Literal>amyloid plaques</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="#UMLS_CUI"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051942</IRI>
        <Literal>C0333463</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="unti:Synonym"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051943</IRI>
        <Literal>CAA</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051943</IRI>
        <Literal>Cerebral_Amyloid_Angiopathy</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="x_1.1:source"/>
            <Literal>UMLS</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051943</IRI>
        <Literal>A heterogeneous group of sporadic or familial disorders characterized by AMYLOID deposits in the walls of small and medium sized blood vessels of CEREBRAL CORTEX and MENINGES. Clinical features include multiple, small lobar CEREBRAL HEMORRHAGE; cerebral ischemia (BRAIN ISCHEMIA); and CEREBRAL INFARCTION. Cerebral amyloid angiopathy is unrelated to generalized AMYLOIDOSIS. Amyloidogenic peptides in this condition are nearly always the same ones found in ALZHEIMER DISEASE. (from Kumar: Robbins and Cotran: Pathologic Basis of Disease, 7th ed., 2005)

From MSH</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="x_1.1:source"/>
            <Literal>UMLS</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051943</IRI>
        <Literal>CAA is less prominently featured (than SPs and NFTs) when considering AD neuropathologic lesions in part because it is variable in severity among AD patients, though when sought diligently it is identified in an estimated 85–95% of AD brains. CAA results from a process whereby the media of parenchymal arterioles, normally composed of smooth muscle cells (SMCs), undergoes progressive loss of these SMCs and simultaneous accumulation of an eosinophilic hyaline material that has the staining properties of amyloid, that is, positivity for thioflavin S or T and congophilia, with the characteristic yellow-green birefringence (of the congophilic material) when a brain section is viewed under polarized light.

https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-pathol-020712-163927</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:exactMatch"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051943</IRI>
        <Literal>Cerebral Amyloid Angiopathy</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="#UMLS_CUI"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051943</IRI>
        <Literal>C0085220</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="unti:Synonym"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051944</IRI>
        <Literal>GVD, of Simchowicz</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051944</IRI>
        <Literal>Granulovacuolar_Degeneration</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="x_1.1:source"/>
            <Literal>UMLS</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051944</IRI>
        <Literal>Describes an abnormality in which the neuronal cytoplasm of hippocampal pyramidal cells is replaced by vacuoles containing small basophilic granules; these may represent an abnormality of neuronal autophagy. Hippocampi showing prominent GVD also often show eosinophilic hyaline rodlike structures—rodlike bodies of Hirano—in the neuropil.

https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-pathol-020712-163927</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:exactMatch"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051944</IRI>
        <Literal>Granulovacuolar degeneration</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="#UMLS_CUI"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051944</IRI>
        <Literal>C0333454; C0333455</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051945</IRI>
        <Literal>Loss_of_Synapse_and_Synaptic_Protein</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051945</IRI>
        <Literal>One of the most important findings in AD brain is loss of synapses and synaptic proteins; this loss can be demonstrated biochemically or by immunohistochemistry using quantitative immunohistochemistry or a Western blot or ELISA assay. It was first shown dramatically in affected cortex when brain sections were immunostained with antibodies against synaptophysin. Quantification and subsequent interpretation of the immunohistochemical signal in such cases must be done by meticulous comparison to brain from a cognitively normal control, because the loss of synaptophysin protein may be subtle on a naked-eye inspection of the tissue section. When synaptic proteins (synaptophysin, synaptotagmin, and rab3a) were compared (by Western blot) among subjects with early- and late-onset AD and vascular dementia, the most profound loss of all synaptic proteins was noted in the early-onset AD subjects; vascular dementia brains showed minimal abnormalities.

https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-pathol-020712-163927</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="unti:Synonym"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051946</IRI>
        <Literal>NFT; Neurofibrillary tangle</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051946</IRI>
        <Literal>Neurofibrillary_Degeneration</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="x_1.1:source"/>
            <Literal>UMLS</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051946</IRI>
        <Literal>Abnormal structures located in various parts of the brain and composed of dense arrays of paired helical filaments (neurofilaments and microtubules). These double helical stacks of transverse subunits are twisted into left-handed ribbon-like filaments that likely incorporate the following proteins: (1) the intermediate filaments: medium- and high-molecular-weight neurofilaments; (2) the microtubule-associated proteins map-2 and tau; (3) actin; and (4) UBIQUITINS. As one of the hallmarks of ALZHEIMER DISEASE, the neurofibrillary tangles eventually occupy the whole of the cytoplasm in certain classes of cell in the neocortex, hippocampus, brain stem, and diencephalon. The number of these tangles, as seen in post mortem histology, correlates with the degree of dementia during life. Some studies suggest that tangle antigens leak into the systemic circulation both in the course of normal aging and in cases of Alzheimer disease.

From MSH</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="x_1.1:source"/>
            <Literal>UMLS</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051946</IRI>
        <Literal>NFTs are dense intraneuronal cytoplasmic basophilic aggregates of filaments that include, on ultrastructural examination, characteristic paired helical filaments. The filaments have a diameter of 10–15 nm and a periodicity of 160 nm. NFTs are almost invariably accompanied by neuropil threads in the adjacent brain parenchyma. Tangles tend to accommodate to the shape and morphology of a given neuron; when present in the pyramidal cell layer of the hippocampus, they appear as elongated parallel skeins of fibrillar intracytoplasmic basophilic material, but when seen in globose or oval neurons, they take on a more disorganized or globular configuration.

https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-pathol-020712-163927</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:exactMatch"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051946</IRI>
        <Literal>Neurofibrillary degeneration</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="#UMLS_CUI"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051946</IRI>
        <Literal>C0085400</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051947</IRI>
        <Literal>Neuron_Death</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051947</IRI>
        <Literal>obsolete neuron death</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="x_1.1:source"/>
            <Literal>UMLS</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051947</IRI>
        <Literal>The process of cell death in a neuron.

From GO</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="#UMLS_CUI"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051947</IRI>
        <Literal>C2754100</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051948</IRI>
        <Literal>Tau_Pathology</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051949</IRI>
        <Literal>Tau_Hyperphosphorylation</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051949</IRI>
        <Literal>Tau becomes hyperphosphorylated and misfolded at both presynaptic and postsynaptic nerve terminals; furthermore, accumulation of hyperphosphorylated tau oligomers (at human synaptic terminals) is associated with increased levels of ubiquitinated substrates and proteasome components, suggesting dysfunction of the ubiquitin-proteasome system.

https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-pathol-020712-163927</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="unti:Function"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051950</IRI>
        <Literal>“Seeds” that can induce pathological conformations of normal peptides; spread disease across brain and accelerated by mutations in APP, PSEN1, and PSEN2</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051950</IRI>
        <Literal>Toxic_Conformation_of_Tau</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11051950</IRI>
        <Literal>Pathological conformations of normal peptides</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11060000</IRI>
        <Literal>Neuroinflammation</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11060000</IRI>
        <Literal>As research into AD has progressed, an addendum has been made to the amyloid cascade hypothesis which inserts neuroinflammation as the causal link between amyloid deposition, tau pathology, and neurodegeneration. Support for this link comes from both human tissue and mouse models, in which activated microglia have been observed surrounding amyloid.

https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/30744705</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="core:definition"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#11060000</IRI>
        <Literal>Neuroinflammation is an inflammatory response in the CNS as a reaction to injury or infection, with the accumulation of glial cells. In this process, cytokines, chemokines, complement and pattern-recognition receptors (PRRs), cellular and molecular immune factors, may activate microglia and astrocytes, which produce pro-inflammatory cytokines, especially IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α, and additional Aβ production. Stimulated microglia in turn activate other microglia and astrocytes.

https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5652018/</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#12000000</IRI>
        <Literal>Pathway</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>http://www.semanticweb.org/fangli/ontologies/2019/2/untitled-ontology-107#12010000</IRI>
